    "questionNumber": 434,
    "question": "Calculated analytical problem: In the reaction: 2Cu+ -> Cu2+ + Cu(s), given E°(Cu+/Cu) = +0.52 V and E°(Cu2+/Cu+) = +0.15 V, the standard cell potential for disproportionation is:",
    "options": {
      "A": "+0.37 V",
      "B": "-0.37 V",
      "C": "+0.67 V",
      "D": "+0.20 V"
    },
    "optionsList": [
      "+0.37 V",
      "-0.37 V",
      "+0.67 V",
      "+0.20 V"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nThermodynamic feasibility of copper(I) disproportionation.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nReduction: Cu+ + e- -> Cu (E° = +0.52 V). Oxidation: Cu+ -> Cu2+ + e- (E° = +0.15 V).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nE°cell = E°(cathode) - E°(anode) = +0.52 - (+0.15) = +0.37 V.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Cathode half-reaction: Cu+ + e- -> Cu (E° = +0.52 V).\n2. Anode half-reaction: Cu+ -> Cu2+ + e- (E° = +0.15 V).\n3. E°cell = E°cathode - E°anode = +0.52 - 0.15 = +0.37 V.\n4. Since E°cell > 0, Cu+ disproportionates spontaneously in aqueous solution.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: +0.37 V\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nA positive cell potential confirms that Cu+ is unstable in aqueous solution and disproportionates into Cu2+ and Cu.",
    "solutionStructured": {
      "concept": "Thermodynamic feasibility of copper(I) disproportionation.",
      "given": "Reduction: Cu+ + e- -> Cu (E° = +0.52 V). Oxidation: Cu+ -> Cu2+ + e- (E° = +0.15 V).",
      "formula": "E°cell = E°(cathode) - E°(anode) = +0.52 - (+0.15) = +0.37 V.",
      "stepByStep": "1. Cathode half-reaction: Cu+ + e- -> Cu (E° = +0.52 V).\n2. Anode half-reaction: Cu+ -> Cu2+ + e- (E° = +0.15 V).\n3. E°cell = E°cathode - E°anode = +0.52 - 0.15 = +0.37 V.\n4. Since E°cell > 0, Cu+ disproportionates spontaneously in aqueous solution.",
      "finalAnswer": "Option A: +0.37 V",
      "whyOtherOptionsWrong": "A positive cell potential confirms that Cu+ is unstable in aqueous solution and disproportionates into Cu2+ and Cu."
    },
